# Line endings
#
# Without this file the working tree decides what lands in the index: a
# checkout on Windows (or an edit through a mounted share) turns files into
# CRLF, git stores them that way, and `git status` reports hundreds of files
# as modified although nothing changed. Normalising here makes the repository
# content independent of the platform it is edited on.
#
# After adding this file, run ONCE:
#     git add --renormalize .
#     git commit -m "chore: normalise line endings"
# That single commit rewrites the endings of every affected file. Do it on a
# clean tree, with no other work in progress.

# Everything git detects as text is stored with LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that must keep CRLF on checkout: cmd.exe misreads LF-only batch files.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# Files that must keep LF: they run inside the Linux container.
*.sh          text eol=lf
entrypoint.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile    text eol=lf

# Compiled translation catalogues are binary — never touch their bytes.
*.mo binary

# Binary assets
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.exe  binary
*.dll  binary
*.zip  binary
*.mfmod binary

# Translation sources stay text (Weblate writes them), but diffing them line
# by line is what makes a .po review readable.
*.po text eol=lf

# Keep GitHub's language stats honest: vendored/minified assets and the
# generated lock file are not hand-written project code.
uv.lock                        linguist-generated=true
src/mediaforge/web/static/*.min.js linguist-vendored=true
